      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "PURPOSE",
            "content": "We characterize the outcomes of pediatric spontaneous pneumomediastinum in the largest series to date and propose a management pathway."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "All patients at our institution with ICD-9 code 518.1 confirmed to have isolated radiographic findings of spontaneous pneumomediastinum between January 2003 and February 2014 were retrospectively reviewed for admission, intensive care unit (ICU) stay, complications, and outcome."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "We identified 96 children with 99 episodes, median age 14.1 years (IQR: 8.7-16.4). Primary symptoms were chest pain, cough, and dyspnea. Most were hospitalized (n=91, 91.9%), with 20 (20.2%) admitted to ICU. Median lengths of stay (LOS) were 1 day (IQR: 1-2) for non-ICU admissions and 3 days (IQR: 2-3) for ICU admissions. The surgical service discharged non-ICU patients 0.94 days earlier than medical services (95% CI 0.38-1.50, p=0.0014). Asthma affected neither LOS nor ICU admission rates. Follow-up imaging, when obtained (n=81, 81.8%), did not alter management. Recurrences occurred in three asthmatics, all after one year. Each was rehospitalized and discharged uneventfully. No patient developed pneumomediastinum-related complications (e.g., pneumothorax, pneumopericardium, or mediastinitis)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Spontaneous pneumomediastinum without associated comorbidities can be managed with expectant outpatient observation without further imaging. Children with asthma should be treated independent of spontaneous pneumomediastinum."
          }
        ]
      },
